.TH ZERODDS-ROUTER 1 "" "ZeroDDS" "ZeroDDS Manual"
.SH NAME
zerodds-router \- DDS routing-service daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zerodds-router run
\fB\-\-routes\fR \fIFILE\fR
[\fB\-\-types\fR \fISHAPES.json\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIYAML\fR]
[\fB\-\-set\fR \fIPATH\fR=\fIVALUE\fR]...
.br
.B zerodds-router validate
\fB\-\-routes\fR \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B zerodds-router
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIYAML\fR]
[\fB\-\-set\fR \fIPATH\fR=\fIVALUE\fR]...
\fB\-\-check\-config\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zerodds-router
is a DDS-to-DDS forwarding daemon: it copies samples between DDS endpoints
that differ in domain, topic, or partition, optionally filtering and
transforming each sample on the way. It is a native in-DDS routing service,
not a protocol bridge \(em both ends speak RTPS.
.PP
Each route declares an
.I input
and an
.I output
endpoint (domain, topic, type name, partitions, QoS). A route may carry a
content
.I filter
(an SQL-like expression that drops non-matching samples) and a field
.I transform
(rename / set-const). A built-in loop guard prevents a sample from being
re-forwarded on a domain that is both an input and an output.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Start the router and forward until SIGINT/SIGTERM.
.TP
.B validate
Parse and validate the config, print the resolved routes, and exit without
joining any domain.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-routes " \fIFILE\fR"
Route configuration. The format is chosen by extension: a
.I .xml
path is parsed as XML, anything else as JSON. Required.
An element, attribute or key the router does not read is refused with its
name (XML also with its line); in JSON, keys beginning with
.B _
are comments.
.TP
.BR \-\-types " \fISHAPES.json\fR"
A JSON array of type shapes describing each route's
.IR type_name ,
e.g. \fB[{ "name": "ns::T", "members": [{ "name": "v", "kind": "u32" }] }]\fR.
Required only for routes that use a
.I filter
or
.IR transform ,
which must understand the sample's fields. Plain raw-byte forwards
(\fItype_name\fR \fBzerodds::RawBytes\fR) need no shapes.
.TP
.BR \-\-config " \fIYAML\fR"
The DDS runtime configuration: base file, with drop-ins in its sibling
\fIYAML\fR.d directory. Without it, the system configuration directory
(\fI/etc/zerodds\fR on Linux) is read. A \fI.json\fR or \fI.xml\fR path
is refused with a pointer to \fB\-\-routes\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-set " \fIPATH\fR=\fIVALUE\fR"
One runtime setting above every file, repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Validate and print the effective runtime configuration with the origin of
every value, then exit (0 valid, 1 invalid).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The config has a top-level
.I name
and a list of
.IR routes .
Each route has
.IR input " and " output
endpoints; optional
.IR filter ", " transform ", "
.I loop_guard
(default true) and
.I preserve_source_timestamp
(default false). An endpoint has
.IR domain ", " topic ", " type_name ", "
optional
.IR partition " and " qos
(reliability, durability, ownership, ...). See
.I /usr/share/zerodds/configs/router.json.example
for a working two-route configuration.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
trigger a graceful shutdown; the per-route metrics (forwarded, dropped by
loop guard, dropped by filter, lifecycle, errors) are printed before exit.
There is no SIGHUP reload \(em edit the config and restart the service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/zerodds/router.json
Default configuration read by the
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/zerodds-router.service
systemd unit.
.TP
.I /usr/share/zerodds/configs/router.json.example
Shipped example configuration.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A clean validate, a clean shutdown, or
.B \-\-check\-config
found the configuration valid.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid route file or configuration value, or a startup failure.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong (no or an unknown command, a route file
given with
.BR \-\-config ),
or a configuration file could not be read or does not parse.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zerodds-admin (1),
.BR zerodds-spy (1)
.SH AUTHOR
ZeroDDS Contributors.
